    <h2 style="color:#1a3a5c; font-size:1.3rem; margin-bottom:16px;">Understanding Your Legal Rights in the United States</h2>
    
    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:16px;">Every American has legal rights that protect them in everyday situations — from dealing with landlords and employers to understanding consumer protections and family law. However, most people find it difficult to access clear, simple legal information when they need it most. Legal Rights Explained was created to solve exactly this problem.</p>

    <h3 style="color:#1a3a5c; font-size:1rem; margin-bottom:10px;">What Are Your Basic Legal Rights?</h3>
    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:16px;">Your legal rights in the United States are protected by federal law, state law, and the Constitution. These rights cover areas including tenant rights, employment rights, consumer rights, and civil rights. Understanding these rights is the first step to protecting yourself in any legal situation.</p>

    <h3 style="color:#1a3a5c; font-size:1rem; margin-bottom:10px;">Tenant Rights Explained Simply</h3>
    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:16px;">As a tenant in the United States, you have the right to a habitable living space, protection from illegal eviction, and the return of your security deposit within a legally defined timeframe. Laws vary by state — for example, California requires landlords to return deposits within 21 days while Alabama allows 35 days. Knowing your state-specific rights is essential.</p>

    <h3 style="color:#1a3a5c; font-size:1rem; margin-bottom:10px;">Employment Rights Every Worker Should Know</h3>
    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:16px;">Federal employment law protects workers from wrongful termination, workplace discrimination, and unpaid wages. The Fair Labor Standards Act guarantees minimum wage and overtime pay. Title VII of the Civil Rights Act protects employees from discrimination based on race, gender, religion, and national origin. Most states also have additional protections that go beyond federal law.</p>

    <h3 style="color:#1a3a5c; font-size:1rem; margin-bottom:10px;">Consumer Rights and Protections</h3>
    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:16px;">American consumers are protected by the Consumer Protection Act, the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act, and numerous state laws. You have the right to a refund for defective products, protection from deceptive advertising, and the right to dispute incorrect charges on your credit report. Small claims court is available in every state for resolving disputes up to a certain dollar amount without needing a lawyer.</p>

    <h3 style="color:#1a3a5c; font-size:1rem; margin-bottom:10px;">How Legal Rights Explained Works</h3>
    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:16px;">Simply select your state from the dropdown menu, type your legal question in plain everyday language, and click Get Simple Answer. Our AI-powered tool will provide a clear, structured answer specific to your state's laws — including a simple explanation, your specific rights, recommended next steps, and an important reminder to consult a licensed attorney for advice specific to your situation.</p>

    <h3 style="color:#1a3a5c; font-size:1rem; margin-bottom:10px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
    
    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#1a3a5c;">Is this website a substitute for a lawyer?</strong><br>No. Legal Rights Explained provides general legal information only. For advice specific to your situation, always consult a licensed attorney in your state.</p>

    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#1a3a5c;">Is this service free?</strong><br>Yes. Legal Rights Explained is completely free to use. No signup or registration is required.</p>

    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:8px;"><strong style="color:#1a3a5c;">Which states are covered?</strong><br>All 50 US states are covered. Simply select your state from the dropdown and get answers specific to your location.</p>

    <p style="color:#555; line-height:1.9; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:0;"><strong style="color:#1a3a5c;">What types of legal questions can I ask?</strong><br>You can ask about tenant rights, employment law, consumer rights, small claims court, family law, criminal rights, and much more. Our tool covers the most common everyday legal situations Americans face.</p>

